2034	## Design Decisions and Trade-offs
2035	
2036	**Why `activatePanel()` uses `panel-${name}` IDs instead of `data-tab` attributes**
2037	
2038	The old code used `data-tab` on buttons and `id="tab-${tab}"` on content divs, with `document.querySelectorAll('.tab-content')` to hide all. The new design uses `data-panel` on buttons and `id="panel-${panel}"` on panels, with `document.querySelectorAll('.tab-panel')` to target panels. This avoids collision with the old `.tab-content` class (which no longer exists) and makes the "show all" / "focus" button logic clean.
2039	
2040	**Why the `improvements-list` uses CSS counters instead of HTML `<li>` numbers**
2041	
2042	The list uses `counter-reset` and `counter-increment` in CSS so the number renders as a large styled pseudo-element in a grid column, separate from the content column. This matches the reference design's numbered-list-with-detail-paragraph pattern cleanly without needing wrapper divs per-number.
2043	
2044	**Why `renderMarkdown` is kept but not used in the new renderers**
2045	
2046	The new schema provides structured data (arrays, tables), not Markdown blobs. `renderMarkdown` is preserved because it is an existing helper that may be useful if the generate skill ever produces hybrid content, and removing it would break any consumer that references it directly.
2047	
2048	**Why `escapeHtml` now uses `String(str)` coercion**
2049	
2050	The original `escapeHtml` assumed string input. In the new renderers, numbers (turn count, token count) are sometimes passed directly. Adding `String(str)` coercion prevents a `replace is not a function` error on numeric values without changing the function's external behavior for strings.
2051	
2052	**Why the sticky tab nav uses `top: calc(var(--tab-nav-height) + 14px)`**
2053	
2054	The top bar is sticky at `z-index: 200`. The tab nav is sticky just below it. The `--tab-nav-height: 48px` token is the top-bar height. The `+14px` is the padding offset. Without this, the tab nav would slide under the top bar.
2055	
2056	**Why `proof-sidebar` uses `position: sticky; top: 120px`**
2057	
2058	120px positions the sidebar below both the sticky top-bar and sticky tab-nav when the user scrolls through the proof panel. This matches the reference design where the artifact inventory stays in view as the user reads the evidence table and excerpts.
